SteamARM: KK_FLOAT_STRICT, how much of SignedZeroInfNanPreserve the MSL keeps

DXVK and vkd3d-proton (dxil-spirv) declare SignedZeroInfNanPreserve for every
DXBC shader, and KosmicKrisp honours it instruction by instruction: precise::
functions (rsqrt, sqrt and sin about 3x Metal's fast ones, pow 15x, fma
1.65x), a relaxed math block around every such instruction, fmin/fmax made to
order signed zeros (3.6x), lerp and fract written out, and KK_WORKAROUND_14's
guards: isnan tests around every float compare (2.6x) and a branch in front
of every multiply, which also keeps it from fusing with an add (2.4x) --
microbenchmarks on the M4 against fast math. 87 % of the 16,931 shaders in
this Mac's KosmicKrisp cache had precise:: in them, with 3.0 M guarded
multiplies and 1.17 M guarded compares. MoltenVK, which CrossOver ships,
compiles every shader with fast math and ignores the request.

KK_FLOAT_STRICT, read when a physical device is made:
  full  (the default, unset) all of it, as before: what conformance needs.
  nan   float compares, fmin/fmax and isfinite/isnormal keep their Inf/NaN
        handling (their relaxed blocks, precise fmin/fmax -- which cost
        nothing -- and KK_WORKAROUND_14's compare guards); signed zeros and
        everything else as fast. spirv_to_nir marks every FMin/FMax as
        preserving infinities whatever the shader declares (Mesa: Unity and
        Unreal Engine break without it); nan keeps that, fast does not.
  fast  none, as MoltenVK: no precise::, no guards, no relaxed blocks; mix
        and fract are Metal's.
NoContraction (nir_fp_exact) keeps its safe math block in every mode.

The flags are dropped in nir_to_msl, after every NIR optimisation has run
with them: NIR's own algebra, and the code nir_opt_varyings moves from a
fragment shader into its vertex shader, are what they were. Only compute
shaders and fragment shaders that do not write depth are relaxed, by the
application's stage (kk_shader.c): vertex shaders, the kernel that runs a
vertex shader before a geometry shader or tessellation, everything made of a
geometry shader and fragment shaders writing depth keep their MSL to the
byte, because whatever computes a position must compute it the same way in
every pipeline it is linked into (patch 18: a depth prepass and a colour
pass with depth test EQUAL). MESA_KK_DEBUG=msl now prints the geometry
shader's variants too.

The mode is part of the disk cache's driver_flags and, when not full, of
pipelineCacheUUID and shaderBinaryUUID: neither Mesa's disk cache nor an
application's saved VkPipelineCache (vkd3d-proton keeps one) hands a
pipeline compiled in one mode to another. The default keys as before.

Checked: tests/kk/vk_float_strict.c (per mode: results on ordinary and
special values; the MSL of every stage, the strict ones byte for byte
against full; both caches) and tests/elf/run_vk_arm64.sh, with
KK_FLOAT_STRICT unset and fast. A lighting loop in a compute shader
(tests/kk/float_strict/bench.comp, 1 M threads x 256 iterations): 28.1 ms
full, 5.4 ms nan, 5.1 ms fast.

--- a/src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
@@ -2423,6 +2423,46 @@ predeclare_ssa_values(struct nir_to_msl_ctx *ctx, nir_function_impl *impl)
    }
